<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Inference with Diameter-based Stopping</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">During inference, we transport the visual features of test images along the predicted geodesic flow. To prevent overshooting at the crowded hyperbolic boundary, we further propose an adaptive strategy comprising two steps:</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Quantitative results.<span id="S3.T1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Performance comparison (§<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Quantitative Comparison ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Path-Decoupled Hyperbolic Flow Matching for Few-Shot Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) of the state-of-the-art approaches. Based on CLIP-LoRA, we add HFM to further improve the performance. Top-1 accuracy averaged over 3 random seeds is reported. The highest value of each dataset is bolded.</span></span></figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.9" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:632.3pt;height:358.5pt;vertical-align:-177.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-35.1pt,19.9pt) scale(0.9,0.9) ;">
